<section class="lp-section">
  <div class="lp-container">
    <h2 class="lp-h2">What we believe</h2>
    <p class="lp-section-lead">The convictions the whole stack is built on.</p>
    <div class="lp-principle-grid">
      <div class="lp-principle">
        <h4>Agents belong to the team, not to a tab</h4>
        <p>
          The personal-copilot model breaks the moment a team moves fast —
          divergent context, duplicated work, conflicts nobody asked to
          reconcile. People think out loud and decide together in one shared
          space, and agents live in that same space: reading the same
          discussion, writing to the same knowledge base, working in the open.
          Nothing to merge.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-principle">
        <h4>Processes are code; judgement is borrowed</h4>
        <p>
          A company's processes should be written down — explicit,
          deterministic, inspectable. An always-on orchestrator runs them and
          spawns an agent only for the parts that genuinely need judgement, then
          tears it down when the task ends. An LLM where no deterministic
          practice exists yet — not for everything.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-principle">
        <h4>Capability is the hard part</h4>
        <p>
          Inviting an agent into a conversation is trivial; making it actually
          <em>do the work</em> is the whole game. That takes a real harness —
          planning, tools, verification, recovery, parallelism — not a chat
          bridge. We reuse the strongest available harness and agent
          implementations instead of shipping a weaker copy of a solved problem.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-principle">
        <h4>Security is structural, not a setting</h4>
        <p>
          Every agent reaches back only through its own auto-generated MCP
          exposing a deny-by-default capability grant. Secrets stay in the
          orchestrator. A prompt injection cannot touch what was never granted.
          Isolation is the architecture.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-principle">
        <h4>Meet people where they already are</h4>
        <p>
          Communication is a separate, pluggable layer, spoken over the tools
          teams already trust — Discourse, Zulip, Telegram, GitHub issues,
          email. An agent asks the team over whatever channel is already in use
          and resumes when the answer lands. The team keeps its home; the agents
          move in.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-principle">
        <h4>Own your stack</h4>
        <p>
          Self-hosted from the metal up. Your processes, your data, your servers
          — no platform sitting in the middle of your agent conversations.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-principle">
        <h4>Reuse, don't reinvent</h4>
        <p>
          Best-in-class harness, mature communication software, proven
          infrastructure primitives. We build the connective tissue and the
          boundaries between them — not worse copies of what already works.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="lp-section">
  <div class="lp-container">
    <h2 class="lp-h2">One stack, top to bottom</h2>
    <p class="lp-section-lead">
      Each layer is independently useful, and each is swappable. Start with one;
      grow into the rest.
    </p>
    <div class="lp-stack">
      <div class="lp-tier lp-tier-comms">
        <div class="lp-tier-name">Communication</div>
        <div class="lp-tier-desc">
          The shared space. People discuss and decide over the channel they
          already use — Discourse, Zulip, Telegram, GitHub issues, email — and
          agents live in that same context.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-conn">↕ messages in · answers out</div>
      <div class="lp-tier lp-tier-zeno">
        <div class="lp-tier-name"><span>zeno</span> — orchestration</div>
        <div class="lp-tier-desc">
          Deterministic processes in Lisp spawn ephemeral, capability-scoped
          agents. An agent can ask the team and resume when the reply lands.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-conn">↕ spawns · tears down</div>
      <div class="lp-tier lp-tier-agents">
        <div class="lp-tier-name">Harness</div>
        <div class="lp-tier-desc">
          The work itself: the strongest available agents, planning, tools,
          verification, and recovery — boxed behind a per-agent capability grant.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-conn">↑ runs on</div>
      <div class="lp-tier lp-tier-nixops">
        <div class="lp-tier-name"><span>castle</span> / <span>nixops</span> — infrastructure</div>
        <div class="lp-tier-desc">
          Your servers as code: reproducible NixOS, encrypted disks,
          rollback-safe deploys, declarative services — self-hosted from the
          metal up.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="lp-section">
  <div class="lp-container">
    <h2 class="lp-h2">What this is not</h2>
    <div class="lp-principle-grid">
      <div class="lp-principle">
        <h4>Not a new chat client</h4>
        <p>We integrate with the ones teams already run; nothing to migrate to.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-principle">
        <h4>Not a personal copilot</h4>
        <p>Team agents in shared context, not per-seat assistants in isolated tabs.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-principle">
        <h4>Not a closed platform</h4>
        <p>Open source and self-hostable end to end, with no third party in the middle.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-principle">
        <h4>Not a monolith</h4>
        <p>Each layer stands alone; the framework is the seams between them.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

